D1 (2026-10-18, after the first output, before any result was shared): three of the first "wrong" edges were errors in
the ground truth, not in Graphify.
- Absolute imports in test and example folders resolve against pytest-style roots, e.g. `from test_itsdangerous.x
  import y` under `tests/`, or `from complex.cli import z` under `examples/complex/`.
- Inside a package, `from types import TracebackType` is the standard library, not the sibling `types.py`.
The fix: absolute imports resolve against the repository root, `src/`, and every ancestor folder of the importing file
that is not itself inside a package (has no `__init__.py`). The importing file's own folder is used only when it is
not a package. Each fix moves edges from "wrong" to "right", in Graphify's favour.
Also added: lists of the imports Graphify missed, and examples of call edges failing the name check, for inspection.

D1, continued (same inspection, before sharing): the graph is not a multigraph, so Graphify keeps one edge per file
pair, and several statements importing the same module from one file share it. Recall therefore counts an internal
import statement as found if any Graphify import edge from that file reaches one of its acceptable targets, on any
line. The call name check also accepts a call into a module by attribute (`warnings.warn(` for a call edge to
`warnings`).

A1 (addition, 2026-10-18): the post asks whether Graphify "got better" since "last month". The same check runs on
Graphify 0.9.64 (PyPI, 2026-09-18) with the same repositories and the same scorer (graphs in graphs_0964/).
